 { Here is another bishop vs. knight ending, this time with the breakthrough being realized by the knight's side. } 1... { -KEY- } Nf6 2. Bf3 $6 ( { White should have preferred } 2. Kd3 { , followed by Ke3, avoiding the game scenario. } ) 
g5 3. Kd3 g4 $1 { Without the minor pieces on the board this breakthrough would have won on the spot, whereas here it just yields Black a pleasant initiative in practice. } 4. hxg4 ( { Obviously, } 4. Bg2 $2 { failed to } Nh5 { with a fork to follow. } ) 
Nh7 $1 { There is no hurry with ...h4-h3, and Barkhagen instead reorganizes his pieces - the king should go to g5 and then the knight back to f6. Incidentally, 'don't hurry' is a good rule of thumb in the endgame, one that Shereshevsky dedicated a whole chapter to in his great work 'Endgame Strategy'. } 5. Ke3 Kf6 6. Be2 Kg5 7. Kf3 Nf6 8. Bc4 $6 { This and the next move are too 'active'. } ( { With } 8. Bf1 { White held the balance - Hecht; as after } Nxg4 9. Bh3 { the threat of 10.Bc8 forces Black into } Nh2 10. Kg2 Ng4 { with a repetition. } ) 
h3 9. Be6 $2 ( 9. Bf1 { was correct, though such a move is psychologically hard of course. Black could react } Kh4 10. g5 h2 $5 11. Bg2 Nh5 { ; e.g. } 12. g6 Kg5 13. g7 Nxg7 14. Kg3 Ne6 15. Kxh2 Kf4 { keeping some practical chances. } ) 
 ( { Stockfish found an even more surprising solution: } 9. Kg3 $1 Nxe4 ( { or } 9. Be6 Nxg4 10. Bf1 $1 h2 11. Bg2 Nf6 12. Kxh2 Kf4 13. Bh3 { heading for c8 } ) 
10. Kxh3 Nxf2 11. Kg3 Nxg4 12. Kf3 { White gave away all their kingside pawns for Black's passed pawn, still they seem to be surviving, for example } Nf6 13. Be6 Nd5 14. a3 { followed by Bc8. } ) 
Nxe4 $1 { Black wins a pawn and, more importantly, enables the deflection of the white king from the h-pawn by ...Kh4 and ...Ng5+ (or ...Nd2+). } 10. Bc8 Kh4 $1 11. Bxb7 Ng5+ ( { In view of } 11... Ng5 12. Ke3 h2 13. Bxc6 e4 $1 { White resigned - Atlas. } ) 

