 { In endings with opposite-coloured bishops the breakthrough is an important resource, as we will see in the next example.With one extra pawn on each flank, and the b-pawn severely limiting the enemy bishop, Black is winning. Nevertheless, the way he achieved his goal is quite instructive. } 1... { -KEY- } e4 $1 { A breakthrough aimed at creating a second passed pawn... on the h-file! } ( { The methodical } 1... Kf4 { followed by ...e5-e4, was also possible; e.g. } 2. Ke2 e4 3. fxe4 fxe4 4. Bc2 g5 5. Bb1 e3 6. Bc2 Kg3 7. Be4 ( { or } 6... e4 6. Kf1 e2 8. Kxe2 Kxg2 { with 9...g4 next; } ) 
Bd4 $1 { (heading for d2) } 8. Kf1 Bc3 9. Ke2 Bd2 10. Kf1 g4 $1 11. hxg4 h3 { (a breakthrough that frees the f3-square for the king) } 12. gxh3 Kf4 13. Bd3 Kf3 { and wins. } ) 
2. fxe4 f4 $1 3. e5 g5 ( { The straightforward } 3... f3 { also worked, since after } 4. gxf3 Kxh3 5. Bxg6 Kg3 { the h-pawn proves unstoppable. We will see a similar picture in the next note. } ) 
4. Bf5 ( { Or } 4. Be4 f3 5. gxf3 Kxh3 { and the h-pawn decides. } ) 
g4 $1 5. e6 ( { Black's point was that after } 5. hxg4 f3 6. gxf3 h3 { the h-pawn again proves unstoppable. The text allows White to queen, but to no avail. } ) 
f3 $1 ( { Another good option was } 5... gxh3 6. gxh3 f3 { with a decisive zugzwang, e.g. } 7. Ke1 Kg2 ) 
6. gxf3 gxh3 7. e7 h2 ( { Not } 7... Bxe7 $2 8. Kg1 { with a draw. } ) 
8. e8=Q h1=Q+ 9. Ke2 Qxf3+ 
