 { Another complex opposite-coloured bishop ending. Black is two pawns down but he has managed to block the enemy pawns quite confidently. However, positions with opposite-coloured bishops contain a lot of dynamics, a fact that I forgot when making my next, lazy move. } 1... { -KEY- } Bc6 $2 ( 1... Be4 { was correct, avoiding what now follows in the game. } ) 
2. e4 $3 { An unexpected breakthrough that creates a powerful passed pawn on the h-file. } Bxe4 ( { Obviously, } 2... fxe4 $2 3. Kxg4 { would make things too easy for White on the kingside. } ) 
3. f3 $1 gxf3 ( { After } 3... Bxf3 $6 4. h4 $1 { , the h-pawn becomes a giant, while Black's own passed pawns can hardly move. There can follow } Ke7 5. h5 Kf7 6. Kg5 Be4 7. h6 Kg8 8. Kf6 { , with Ke7-d6 next, and the c-pawn decides. The text is more resilient due to the advanced passed pawn that emerges on f3. } ) 
4. Bd4 $1 { White's little endgame combination is concluded, and we can see that the two pawns were well invested. On the g1-a7 diagonal the bishop doesn't just control the f3-pawn, it also protects the one on c5; besides, it will parry the future advance of the pawns on e6 and f5. More on the idea of one single diagonal in Chapter Four. } Bd5 { Parrying the positional threat of 5 g4. } 5. Ke5 ( { Another promising, perhaps winning option was } 5. Kg5 $5 e5 6. Bf2 Ke6 7. h4 { - P.Flores. } ) 
Ke7 ( { The task of controlling the h-pawn must be assigned to the king; e.g. } 5... Bc4 6. h4 Be2 { failed to the simple } 7. Bf2 ) 
6. h4 Ba8 { A waiting move. } ( { After } 6... Kf7 $2 7. Kd6 { , followed by 8 c6, White wins immediately, so it makes sense to postpone the further king transfer until the h-pawn is more exposed. } ) 
7. h5 Bd5 8. Be3 $1 { Some useful prophylaxis, so that a future ...e6-e5 won't be effected with tempo. } Kf7 ( { I couldn't wait any longer, since } 8... Ba8 9. h6 Kf7 10. Kd6 { just leads to a worse version of the game. } ) 
9. Kd6 Kf6 $1 { Preparing ...e6-e5, the only chance for counterplay. } 10. c6 Bxc6 $6 ( { I had already spotted the defensive idea and was eager to carry it out, but } 10... Bc4 { was more tenacious, postponing the bishop sacrifice. Here White would have to play quite precisely in order to score the full point: } 11. Kc5 $1 { (heading for the a4-pawn) } ( { in contrast, after } 10... Bxc6 10. c7 $6 Ba6 12. Kd7 e5 13. c8=Q $11 Bxc8 14. Kxc8 f4 $1 15. gxf4 exf4 16. Bf2 Kg5 17. Kc7 Kxh5 18. Kd6 Kg4 19. Ke5 Kh3 $5 20. Kxf4 Kg2 21. Ke3 Kf1 $1 { , he can't make progress without sacrificing the bishop for the f-pawn, after which Black's king gets to the c-file in time } ) 
Ba6 12. Kb4 e5 13. Kxa4 f4 14. gxf4 exf4 15. Bf2 Kg5 16. Ka5 Bc8 17. Kb6 Kxh5 18. a4 { and the white pawns are faster. } ) 
